1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,902
Day one hundred forty-seven of a Mars transit.

2
00:00:03,902 --> 00:00:08,650
A message to Earth takes up to twenty-two minutes, one way.

3
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:13,800
So when an astronaut's body starts to change, who notices first?

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,500
Our challenge: health monitoring software for astronauts on space missions.

5
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:20,921
We are Team AstroBone from Bangladesh.

6
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:25,134
I'm Mysunat Islam, biomedical engineer and team lead.

7
00:00:25,134 --> 00:00:28,712
Redwan Ahamed Tamim is our lead data analyst,

8
00:00:28,712 --> 00:00:33,800
and Mashyiat Islam Borno designs everything you're about to see.

9
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:37,612
We've written about twenty-nine thousand lines of code,

10
00:00:37,612 --> 00:00:40,961
two hundred fifty-seven tests and forty-one documents,

11
00:00:40,961 --> 00:00:45,800
and turned fourteen hundred NASA life-science records into one working system.

12
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:50,079
NASA names five hazards of spaceflight: radiation, isolation, distance,

13
00:00:50,079 --> 00:00:52,757
altered gravity and a hostile closed environment.

14
00:00:52,757 --> 00:00:56,800
They hit four systems: immune, bone, cardiovascular and behavioral health.

15
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:02,560
Meet Commander Elena Torres, a fictional astronaut on day one forty-seven,

16
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:04,877
with no flight surgeon on board.

17
00:01:04,877 --> 00:01:09,730
This is her digital twin: her skeleton changing across the mission,

18
00:01:09,730 --> 00:01:14,800
the muscles that protect it, and a heart beating at her recorded rate.

19
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:19,448
And the twin's main characters are NASA's own life-science data,

20
00:01:19,448 --> 00:01:22,169
from the Ames Life Sciences Data Archive.

21
00:01:22,169 --> 00:01:25,421
Study 804: after just thirty-seven days in space,

22
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:29,934
mice lost fifty-five percent of trabecular bone volume in the femur.

23
00:01:29,934 --> 00:01:34,911
Inspiration4 crew data show immune and blood markers shifting after flight.

24
00:01:34,911 --> 00:01:38,030
Two more studies cover the heart and radiation.

25
00:01:38,030 --> 00:01:42,079
AstroBone carries fourteen hundred of these records on board,

26
00:01:42,079 --> 00:01:44,800
each verified by its SHA-256 fingerprint.

27
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:47,267
Here's how the data protects her.

28
00:01:47,267 --> 00:01:49,334
Every day, four kinds of signals.

29
00:01:49,334 --> 00:01:52,465
AstroBone compares each one with her own baseline,

30
00:01:52,465 --> 00:01:55,973
and NASA's life-science data tells it what to watch for.

31
00:01:55,973 --> 00:01:59,042
When something drifts, she gets a safe next step,

32
00:01:59,042 --> 00:02:02,800
and her medical and exercise team sees exactly what changed.

33
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,336
Here's the engine, in slow motion.

34
00:02:05,336 --> 00:02:07,912
MediaPipe tracks the body on this device,

35
00:02:07,912 --> 00:02:12,183
and the musculoskeletal twin copies the squat with its feet planted.

36
00:02:12,183 --> 00:02:15,638
From one squat AstroBone measures knee range of motion,

37
00:02:15,638 --> 00:02:18,026
the left-right gap and full extension:

38
00:02:18,026 --> 00:02:22,800
the same signals the daily check compares with the astronaut's own baseline.

39
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:26,996
Now Elena's daily self-check in her digital twin.

40
00:02:26,996 --> 00:02:32,419
Her first capture is badly framed, so AstroBone refuses to compare it.

41
00:02:32,419 --> 00:02:33,581
She repeats it.

42
00:02:33,581 --> 00:02:36,602
With her reaction test, sleep and mood,

43
00:02:36,602 --> 00:02:42,800
three domains move outside her personal range, and the twin shows exactly where.

44
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:48,680
If something unexpected happens, like a loose tool striking her shin,

45
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:52,413
AstroBone reconstructs the impact with physics,

46
00:02:52,413 --> 00:02:55,273
maps bending stress along the tibia,

47
00:02:55,273 --> 00:02:59,800
and runs ten thousand Monte Carlo simulations in seconds.

48
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:03,548
And it all works with no connection.

49
00:03:03,548 --> 00:03:10,987
The pose AI, the NASA life-science evidence pack and the 3D models are cached on

50
00:03:10,987 --> 00:03:12,010
the device.

51
00:03:12,010 --> 00:03:14,800
No health data ever leaves it.

52
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:17,184
That changes who notices first.

53
00:03:17,184 --> 00:03:21,728
She sees a change at the next daily check, not the next ground contact.

54
00:03:21,728 --> 00:03:26,528
Her medical and exercise team sees whether her countermeasures are working.

55
00:03:26,528 --> 00:03:31,648
And on Earth, the same tool can serve rural clinics in Bangladesh and anyone far

56
00:03:31,648 --> 00:03:32,800
from a specialist.

57
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:37,425
What we need next: a validation study against a clinical goniometer,

58
00:03:37,425 --> 00:03:42,395
a pilot in an analog mission, access to restricted long-duration crew records in

59
00:03:42,395 --> 00:03:46,495
NASA's Life Sciences Data Archive to calibrate personal baselines,

60
00:03:46,495 --> 00:03:47,800
and wearable sensors.

61
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:48,917
AstroBone.

62
00:03:48,917 --> 00:03:49,491
Predict.

63
00:03:49,491 --> 00:03:50,064
Observe.

64
00:03:50,064 --> 00:03:50,566
Update.

65
00:03:50,566 --> 00:03:51,140
Protect.

66
00:03:51,140 --> 00:03:55,800
Because on the way to Mars, the first responder is the astronaut.
